INT. LAMPLIGHTER'S TOWER - SPIRAL STAIR - DUSK

1887. A narrow brick tower with an iron ladder. Gas pipes along the walls. A hand-cranked lantern winch. Below, London goes blue.

EZEKIEL FROST, 50s, lamplighter, oilskin coat, strong arms, climbs a few steps and stops. His wife ABIGAIL, 40s, in a shawl, shoves a basket of oil flasks up behind him. Their son TOBY, 12, slips past both, scrambling up the ladder with a wick-trimmer in his teeth.

EZEKIEL
Toby, come back down.

TOBY
I'm nearly there, Papa.

ABIGAIL
Ezekiel, the boy has a head for it.

EZEKIEL
The boy has a head for falling.

ABIGAIL
Let him light the first.

EZEKIEL
He is twelve.

ABIGAIL
You were nine.

EZEKIEL
And I fell.

ABIGAIL
And you got back up.

Toby clambers onto the top landing. Ezekiel and Abigail both climb after him, jostling on the narrow stair. At the top, the three of them stand shoulder to shoulder before a great brass lantern, unlit, its glass cold.

TOBY
How does it go?

EZEKIEL
Wick up. Valve open. Spark.

TOBY
Where's the spark?

ABIGAIL
In the flint. Strike it.

Toby takes the flint. Lifts it. Ezekiel catches his wrist.

EZEKIEL
Gently.

TOBY
I know.

EZEKIEL ^
Gently, son.

ABIGAIL ^
Gently, Toby.

He strikes. A spark leaps. The wick catches. A blue-white flower unfolds behind the glass, then yellow, then a steady gold. Light pours across the rooftops of the district.

All three step back. Ezekiel's hand drops onto Toby's shoulder. Abigail's hand drops onto Ezekiel's.

TOBY
Papa. Look.

EZEKIEL
I see it.

Across the city, in the dusk, other lamps are blooming one by one in answer. Toby stares as if he made them all.

ABIGAIL
You did that.

TOBY
I lit one.

ABIGAIL
That's how it starts.

Ezekiel wipes his eyes with the back of his hand and pretends it was the smoke.
